{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact fe418e89900cb87500ca2306f3ec8d5aca3f4043, all 47 changed paths versus main 6477b73, S5 results note first. Reviewed in a separate detached checkout: npm ci, npm ci --prefix experiments/pds, npm run check (0), npm run test:flows -- --group evolution (27/27), npm test (269/269, 0 skipped), tree clean afterwards. Every sourceHashes entry in dynamic, evolution-runtime, evolution, host-flows, participation, pds, protocol and runtime matches HEAD; feasibility.json differs only on package.json and the lockfile and is declared stage-local; all 21 profile sources match and the engine CID is unchanged; the S1 vectors file is byte-identical to the previous candidate; the refreshed S3 CARs load under the current profile.\n\nThe remaining required change of verdict d16125c3 is closed. Sync now transports an available signed closure under a separate 16 MiB budget through readClosure, collectClosure and writeClosure while definition admission keeps 512 KiB, the browser and CLI consume that transport and judge the definition independently, and a closure that cannot be transported makes sync unavailable rather than silently omitting it. The new runtime regression builds two valid 300 KiB definitions, signs a mixed closure over 512 KiB and asserts identical projections on a full reader and a closure-only reader with following entries; reverting either the reader or the collector bound to 512 KiB turns it red. The real-PDS regression stages both definitions, confirms sync carries a candidate over 512 KiB, replays the closure-only sync to the host's own projection file, continues with the next CLI action at position 11, opens a fresh identity-free browser that shows the invalid activation and the later applied entry, and survives a host restart. My own probes agree across 600 KiB and 15 MiB closures, 64 and 65 CIDs, and blocks corrupt, missing or over 512 KiB on one reader, which pause that reader only. The bytes() change delegates to the atcute encoder and produced identical unpadded output to the old encoder over 670 samples from empty to 120,000 bytes, where the old encoder overflowed at 600 KiB. The dead closure comparison is gone, the CLI gate uses expected, and the transient-fault trade-off and restore behaviour are stated in docs/evolution.md.\n\nNon-blocking, for S6 or later. A granted key can still make sync unavailable for every client by naming an available closure whose raw total exceeds 16 MiB, or by accumulating candidates past the shared transport budget or the 2,048-block pool; the host records invalid_activation while clients see a 503 or a pool refusal. That fails loudly and is documented as an operational limit, and a grantee can already pause an app by naming a never-published CID, so it is not a new capability; still, an over-bound closure is invalid from its block sizes alone and could be judged without transport, which would remove the cliff. None of the three 503 paths has a pinning test. docs/evolution.md says an individual closure may use the whole transport budget, but the budget is shared with the initial CAR. The client body limit of 32 MiB leaves little headroom over a 16 MiB CAR in base64 plus 20,000 entries. Every sync re-collects every candidate closure from the PDS, now up to 16 MiB per refresh; caching by closure identity is a small change. The definition alias on CLI compare and stage is now exercised by no test. ApplicationHost.restore still fails every app on the first old-profile app, as the contract now states.\n\nArchitecture: definition, host and client layers; the transport bound is separate from the definition bound and documented in docs/definitions.md and docs/evolution.md; the profile CID pins the corrected source. Security: both verifiers now judge the same signed bytes, the pause paths remain limited to missing or corrupt named content, and the residual cliff is a loud unavailability reachable only by a grant holder. Simplification: judge over-bound closures from sizes without transport, and cache collected closures. Nothing here adopts the plan-review recommendations.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:7916be5fc3b0364d4245394a94842940c70c6b42","head":"fe418e89900cb87500ca2306f3ec8d5aca3f4043","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:53c1cb566e481cab0f38b4c8dbb8952f2fe615c4\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:fa8d62ed900d7697380a68652abb3e45d950a677#git:sha1:e8fd57d9b724b83ebb36d324a703bd5608248e55","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}